      "content": "Above a pH of 7.5, iron in the soil tends to precipitate as iron carbonate (FeCO3) and hematite (Fe2O3), becoming less available to the grapevines. Manganese also tends to form insoluble manganese carbonate (MnCO3) and manganese oxides like hematite (MnO2), reducing its bioavailability. This conversion of iron and manganese into insoluble forms occurs due to the increasing presence of carbonates, which effectively sequester and precipitate the micronutrients from solution, becoming a common nutrient limitation issue in alkaline soils.",
